<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- SplitTemporalToggle — ONE bar key carrying a mode AND that mode's settings window: the square is a mode
     toggle, and a full-height SIDE CAR on its right edge is a latch on the window. Three of these are the
     bar's centre cluster (Controls/Composites/TemporalToggles).

       MODE OFF              MODE ON               MODE ON + WINDOW OPEN
      ┌──────────┬───┐      ┏━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━┓      ┏━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━┓     │ = hairline divider
      │    ↺     │ · │      ┃▓▓▓ ↺ ▓▓▓▓┃ · ┃      ┃▓▓▓ ↺ ▓▓▓▓┃█·█┃     ━ = accent stroke
      │ PastCast │ · │      ┃▓PastCast▓┃ · ┃      ┃▓PastCast▓┃█·█┃     ▓ = elevated face
      │          │ · │      ┃          ┃ · ┃      ┃          ┃█·█┃     █ = ACCENT fill
      └──────────┴───┘      ┗━━━━━━━━━━┻━━━┛      ┗━━━━━━━━━━┻━━━┛
        car DEAD              car LIVE              car LATCHED
      (no mode running,     (click to open        (click to close the window;
       so no window          the window)           the mode is untouched)
       to open)

     ⚠️ THE SPLIT IS VERTICAL, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A key's HEIGHT is already spent — the glyph-and-name
     stack owns it — so anything carved out of the height is a sliver, and anything carved out of the WIDTH
     can be full size. HISTORY: this was a FOOT RAIL across the bottom of the square, and at a 64px key that
     rail was 15px tall holding a 9.5px mark: the smallest thing in the bar, on the thinnest target in it.
     Turned 90° the same idea gets the key's whole height — a 28x72 target and a 24px mark. Don't turn it
     back; the geometry, not the styling, is what was wrong.

     ⚠️ TWO CONTRACTS IN ONE KEY. The square says what is on the MAP (lit = this mode is running); the car
     says what is on the SCREEN (filled = this mode's window is open).
     HISTORY, so this is not half-undone: the three mode keys ONCE meant both things at once — a click turned
     the mode on when unlit and flipped that mode's window when lit — and the window's open state appeared
     NOWHERE on the key, so one click did two different things depending on state the user could not see.
     That was fixed by taking the windows off the keys entirely: three panels became TABS of one Timeframe
     window behind a shared three-dot key. This control fixes the SAME bug the other way round — the key
     keeps its window and grows a second control to hold it. What that buys back is exactly what the tabs
     cost: NowCast and ForeCast coexist as modes, and their panels can sit on two monitors again. Do not
     re-merge the halves; the overloading was the bug, not the pairing.

     ⚠️ THE TWO HALVES ARE SEPARATE GRID COLUMNS, NOT OVERLAPPING SIBLINGS. They hover and light
     INDEPENDENTLY, and the only way to guarantee that is for neither to sit inside the other's bounds. An
     earlier version had the mode key filling the whole key with the settings control drawn ON TOP of it —
     which read correctly but behaved wrongly: hover leaked between them (a disabled overlay is not
     hit-testable, so the pointer fell straight through and lit the whole key) and the floating control's
     fill escaped the key's bounds. Two columns, no overlap, no leak. Do not go back to stacking them.

     ⚠️ THAT COSTS THE MODE HALF ITS SHARED STYLE, and the three Borders below are what pay for it. A key
     divided in two cannot use BarChromeButtonStyle, whose whole job is drawing ONE stroke around ONE square
     — applied to half a key it would draw a box around the left half. So the pieces are split out:
       • FACE  — the key's ground, spanning both columns. Elevated when the mode is on; that is what makes
                 the car part of a LIT key instead of a dark strip beside one.
       • the two halves — fills only (hover / pressed / accent), each rounded on its own outer corners.
       • SHELL — the stroke, spanning both columns, drawn OVER everything and never hit-tested, so it wraps
                 the whole key as one object and thickening it to the accent nudges nothing.
     ⚠️ Both Borders MUST stay IsHitTestVisible="False" — either one would otherwise swallow the pointer and
     neither half would ever hover.
     ⚠️ Known trade, accepted: the shared style brightens a key's STROKE on hover, and the shell cannot see
     hover (it is not the thing being pointed at). The face wash carries hover here instead.

     ⚠️ THE CAR IS DISABLED WHILE THE MODE IS OFF, and the view model closes a window whose mode has just
     stopped (MapViewModel.OnTemporalModesChanged). So an unlit car can never be hiding an open window,
     which is what lets it read as a STATE rather than merely as a button.

     ⚠️ ACCENT MEANS TWO THINGS INSIDE THIS ONE KEY: the STROKE means "this mode is on", the CAR FILL means
     "this mode's window is open". Everywhere else in the bar accent means only the former. It is deliberate
     here — two controls, two contracts, so two places for the colour — and it makes the car the second
     accent in the bar that is not a plain "this key is lit" (the pane key's anchor cell was the first).

     ⚠️ THIS KEY IS NOT A SQUARE, and it is one of only two that aren't. Its MODE HALF is the shared
     BarKeySide square, so its glyph and name match Sites and Settings exactly; the car is added beside it,
     making the key BarKeySide + BarSideCarWidth wide. (The other exception is the pane key, whose mark is a
     wide picture of the map band.) Nothing here sets that total: the two halves declare their own widths
     and the Grid adds them up, so the number cannot drift out of step with its parts.

     ⚠️ THE MARK IS DRAWN, NOT A FONT GLYPH — three dots, stacked vertically along the car. An ellipsis is
     the one mark that says "there is more behind this" without having to be learned, and it avoids
     borrowing the gear that already means the app-wide Settings window on the bar's right edge. Drawing it
     removes the verify-the-codepoint risk entirely (a wrong one ships as an empty box). Authored in a 6x26
     box; the Viewbox is given only a HEIGHT and takes its width from that box, so the shape owns its aspect.

     SIZING is entirely declarative — Controls/Styles.xaml section 2 for the shared numbers, this file's own
     Resources for the mark. There is NO code-behind sizing here and none anywhere else in the bar; see the
     ⚠️ history in that section for the class that used to compute it all. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Primitives.SplitTemporalToggle"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!-- ⚠️ Both styles are LOCAL, not in Controls/Styles.xaml: this control is their only consumer, and
             the house rule there is that a style earns its place by having more than one. They are also NOT
             BasedOn the bar-key chain — see the ⚠️ in the header on why a divided key cannot use it.
             What they DO share with it is the vocabulary: the OverlayBarSurface* faces in the same
             resting → raised order, and accent reserved for "this one is on". -->

        <!-- The MODE half. Fills only — the FACE behind it supplies the elevated ground when the mode is on,
             and the SHELL over it supplies the stroke.
             ⚠️ Checked is deliberately TRANSPARENT rather than an elevated fill: the face is already
             elevated by then, and painting it again here would double up. Same for CheckedPressed, which
             matches the shared style's behaviour (its checked and checked-pressed faces were identical too,
             so a press on an already-lit key has never had its own colour). -->
        <Style x:Key="ModeHalfStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8,0,0,8"/>
            <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource BarKeySide}"/>
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource BarKeySide}"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="UseSystemFocusVisuals" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                        <Grid>
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal"/>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceHoverBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceElevatedBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="Checked">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceElevatedHoverBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedDisabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Content.Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

                            <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                    Background="Transparent"
                                    CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"/>

                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Content"
                                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                              VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                                              Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                        </Grid>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- The SIDE CAR: the settings latch down the key's right edge.
             ⚠️ Its hover face is the ELEVATED hover, not the plain one. The car is only interactive while
             the mode is on, and an on key's face is already the elevated surface — hovering to the plain
             hover brush would go DARKER and read as the key switching off.
             ⚠️ Its HEIGHT comes from a vertical stretch, never a value: the mode half beside it is what
             makes the key that tall, and a second opinion on the height could only ever disagree. -->
        <Style x:Key="SideCarStyle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0,8,8,0"/>
            <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource BarSideCarWidth}"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="UseSystemFocusVisuals" Value="True"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                        <Grid>
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal"/>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceElevatedHoverBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceElevatedBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <!-- Disabled keeps the divider and dims only the mark (the code-behind
                                         does that): the car must still READ as part of the key when the mode
                                         is off, or the key would change shape every time a mode starts. -->
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled"/>

                                    <!-- ON = the window is open. Accent fills the car and takes the divider
                                         with it, so it reads as one solid block rather than as a fill with a
                                         line ruled down the side of it. -->
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Checked">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedPressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorTertiaryBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorTertiaryBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>

                                    <!-- Unreachable in practice — a mode stopping closes its window, so the
                                         car is unchecked before it is disabled. Kept so the list is total. -->
                                    <VisualState x:Name="CheckedDisabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

                            <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                    Background="Transparent"
                                    BorderBrush="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolidBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1,0,0,0"
                                    CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"/>

                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Content"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Grid>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- Two Auto columns: each half declares its own width and the Grid adds them up, so the key's total
         width has no third opinion that could drift away from its parts. -->
    <Grid x:Name="KeyRoot">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- the mode half: the shared square -->
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- the side car -->
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- ⚠️ THE FACE, THE STROKE AND THE MARK'S INK ARE STATES, NOT CODE — AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS FIX,
             not tidying. They used to be assigned in the code-behind from
             Application.Current.Resources.TryGetValue, which resolves against Application.RequestedTheme —
             the OS theme, settable only before content loads. The app pins its palette on the ROOT ELEMENT's
             RequestedTheme instead (see MainWindow.ApplyAppTheme), so every {ThemeResource} in XAML resolved
             correctly while those code lookups kept handing back the OTHER theme's brush: on a dark-mode
             machine the light theme's lit key came back with a near-BLACK elevated face and white text.
             Symmetric and latent — a light-mode machine had the mirror of it in the dark theme.
             Here the framework resolves against this element's ActualTheme, and re-resolves on a live
             switch, which the code path could not do (it only re-ran when a DP changed).
             ⚠️ Do NOT reintroduce a brush lookup in the code-behind for anything that varies by theme.
             The two `double` lookups elsewhere in the app are fine — a size has no theme. -->
        <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

            <!-- Is the MODE on? Drives the key's whole ground + stroke. Both states set every value: a
                 property left unset in one branch keeps whatever the other branch wrote. -->
            <VisualStateGroup x:Name="ModeStates">
                <VisualState x:Name="ModeOff">
                    <VisualState.Setters>
                        <!-- Transparent rather than a theme key: "no fill" is not a colour that varies —
                             an unlit key shows the bar's own surface through it in either theme. -->
                        <Setter Target="Face.Background" Value="Transparent"/>
                        <Setter Target="Shell.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="Shell.BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
                    </VisualState.Setters>
                </VisualState>
                <VisualState x:Name="ModeOn">
                    <VisualState.Setters>
                        <!-- The FACE spans both halves, so a lit key reads as one object rather than as a
                             lit square with a dark strip beside it. The halves' own template states paint
                             over this on hover and press. -->
                        <Setter Target="Face.Background" Value="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceElevatedBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="Shell.BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="Shell.BorderThickness" Value="2"/>
                    </VisualState.Setters>
                </VisualState>
            </VisualStateGroup>

            <!-- The three-dot mark's ink. Shapes do not inherit the content Foreground the templates drive,
                 so it is addressed here — and it has THREE values, not two: dimmed while the car is dead,
                 the usual secondary text colour while it is live, and the on-accent colour once the car
                 fills, where the ordinary ink would sit on a saturated ground and disappear.
                 ⚠️ TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush, never a hardcoded dark: the accent is light in the
                 dark theme and dark in the light one, so the ink on top has to flip with it. -->
            <VisualStateGroup x:Name="MarkStates">
                <VisualState x:Name="MarkDead">
                    <VisualState.Setters>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot1.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot2.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot3.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorDisabledBrush}"/>
                    </VisualState.Setters>
                </VisualState>
                <VisualState x:Name="MarkLive">
                    <VisualState.Setters>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot1.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot2.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot3.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                    </VisualState.Setters>
                </VisualState>
                <VisualState x:Name="MarkOnAccent">
                    <VisualState.Setters>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot1.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot2.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                        <Setter Target="MarkDot3.Fill" Value="{ThemeResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                    </VisualState.Setters>
                </VisualState>
            </VisualStateGroup>

        </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

        <!-- FACE — the key's ground, under both halves. Elevated when the mode is on; that is what makes the
             car part of a lit key rather than a dark strip beside one. -->
        <Border x:Name="Face" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                CornerRadius="8"
                Background="Transparent"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"/>

        <ToggleButton x:Name="ModeKey" Grid.Column="0"
                      Style="{StaticResource ModeHalfStyle}"
                      Click="OnModeKeyClick">
            <Grid x:Name="ModeContent" Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- glyph -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- name -->
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <FontIcon x:Name="ModeGlyph" Grid.Row="0"
                          FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                          FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyGlyphSize}"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="NameText" Grid.Row="1"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyNameSize}"/>
            </Grid>
        </ToggleButton>

        <ToggleButton x:Name="CarKey" Grid.Column="1"
                      Style="{StaticResource SideCarStyle}"
                      ToolTipService.ToolTip="Open settings for this temporal mode"
                      Checked="OnCarToggled"
                      Unchecked="OnCarToggled">
            <!-- Three dots, stacked VERTICALLY, authored in a 6x26 box. The Viewbox is given only a
                 HEIGHT, so the shape's own box decides its width and the aspect can never be stated wrongly
                 in two places.
                 ⚠️ VERTICAL because the car is: a tall narrow column wants a tall narrow mark, and dots
                 running along it read as belonging to it rather than as a label pasted across it.
                 ⚠️ THIS IS THE OLD PANEL KEY'S MARK, and that is deliberate. The retired three-dot key
                 beside this trio opened the settings panel; the ellipsis is the one mark that says "there
                 is more behind this" without having to be learned, which is exactly what the car does. It
                 has simply moved inside the key whose settings it opens.
                 ⚠️ The brushes are set by the code-behind, not here: Shapes do not inherit the content
                 Foreground the template's states drive, and this ink has to flip to a dark colour when the
                 car fills with accent. -->
            <Viewbox x:Name="MarkBox" Stretch="Uniform" Height="{StaticResource BarSideCarMarkHeight}">
                <Canvas Width="6" Height="26">
                    <Ellipse x:Name="MarkDot1" Width="6" Height="6" Canvas.Left="0" Canvas.Top="0"/>
                    <Ellipse x:Name="MarkDot2" Width="6" Height="6" Canvas.Left="0" Canvas.Top="10"/>
                    <Ellipse x:Name="MarkDot3" Width="6" Height="6" Canvas.Left="0" Canvas.Top="20"/>
                </Canvas>
            </Viewbox>
        </ToggleButton>

        <!-- SHELL — the stroke, over both halves. Never hit-tested, so it cannot swallow the pointer that
             the halves need in order to hover independently. -->
        <Border x:Name="Shell" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                CornerRadius="8"
                BorderThickness="1"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
